{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChQUzpbatk7cRMYQMA1qy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Got it. Customers pick the plan and choose 2, 3, or 4 equal installments. That changes the plan from my recommendation that the business sets it, and it raises some new questions:\n\n**Follow-ups to this answer**\n- **How often?** Is monthly still right, with the first installment due on the invoice's current due date?\n- **Leftover cents:** $100 doesn't divide evenly by 3. Is it OK to add the leftover cent to the last installment ($33.33, $33.33, $33.34)?\n- **When can a customer choose?** My suggestion: only while the invoice is `sent`, not overdue, and nothing has been paid yet. Once chosen, the plan can't be changed. Should every invoice be eligible, or only some (for example, above a minimum amount)?\n\n**Still open from before.** If you don't mind them, I'll go with my recommendations:\n- **#3 Payments:** each payment goes to the oldest unpaid installment first, and anything extra rolls forward.\n- **#4 Overdue:** the invoice is `overdue` while any installment is late and goes back to `sent` once the customer catches up. The alternative is that one missed installment makes the whole balance due immediately.\n- **#5 Late fees:** 2% of the missed installment, charged once for each missed one. Separately, should I fix the existing gap where the late fee is never actually added to what the customer owes?\n- **#6 Reminders:** one reminder 3 days before each installment is due.\n\nOnce you've answered these, I'll write it up and build it with tests."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1435,"cache_read_input_tokens":31334,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1435},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformati